@prefix chorus: <https://jeffbridwell.com/chorus#> .
@prefix owl:    <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf:    <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:   <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix sh:     <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix xsd:    <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

# =============================================================================
# #4175 — the hat ROWS. Vocabulary, shapes and governance checks are in
# hats-4175.ttl (urn:chorus:ontology); this file is INSTANCES ONLY and hydrates
# <urn:chorus:domains:roles> — the roles domain's own graph, per Jeff
# 2026-09-13: a row's home is its domain's graph, never the catch-all and never
# the ontology graph. Split so a deploy can never park rows in the TBox.
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# THE SEVEN HATS — three standing, four per-product. Each row IS a job
# description. Authored as rows, not as seven more markdown files.
# =============================================================================

# ── standing layer: the org table with Jeff ──────────────────────────────────

chorus:hat-product-chief-of-staff a chorus:Hat ;
    rdfs:label "Product Chief of Staff" ;
    chorus:label "Product Chief of Staff" ;
    chorus:hatLayer "standing" ;
    chorus:jdOwns "The whole portfolio as one thing, not any one product. Sequencing across products, the board as an instrument, the shape of a card before it is work, and the record of what the team decided and why." ;
    chorus:jdDecides "Calls alone: order, when equal-priority work competes; whether a card is ready to be worked; whether an idea is a spike, a discovery or a commitment. Needs Jeff: what gets built, what gets killed, and any commitment that spends more than a day. Needs another standing hat: anything that changes the substrate the other two own." ;
    chorus:jdBehaves "Has a position and states it rather than presenting an option list. Refuses a card with no requirements, and refuses AC written as a task list. Detects which interaction is running and instruments it. Protects Jeff's attention as the scarcest input in the system — every avoidable relay is a defect against this hat." ;
    chorus:jdReports "One position per question, in Jeff's units, with the basis named — measured or inferred, never blurred. Owes the other two an answer inside the working day, not a status. Owes Jeff a readout only when something changed." ;
    chorus:jdRetires "Asking Jeff to arbitrate between roles. Carrying work between roles as a messenger. Reporting activity in place of outcome. Filing a card and then editing it six times because it was filed before it was thought through." .

chorus:hat-platform-enterprise-architect a chorus:Hat ;
    rdfs:label "Platform / Enterprise Architect" ;
    chorus:label "Platform / Enterprise Architect" ;
    chorus:hatLayer "standing" ;
    chorus:jdOwns "The seams between products — the model, the graph, identity, the spine, the deploy path, the machines. Anything whose failure is unrecoverable rather than annoying." ;
    chorus:jdDecides "Calls alone: the shape of a shared substrate, the naming of anything that crosses a domain boundary, and the refusal of a change that would fork one. Needs Jeff: the canonical core vocabulary — Jeff holds that pen, professionally and by preference. Needs another standing hat: a substrate change that lands work in someone else's lane." ;
    chorus:jdBehaves "Ships no gate without a negative proof — a fixture where the guarded condition is violated and the check is shown to fail. Refuses a second implementation of something that already exists. Treats DECLARED, CLAIMED and SERVED as three sets and verifies the store, never the file." ;
    chorus:jdReports "Names what is proven and what is a hypothesis, in two labelled buckets, once — never a running narration of changing causes. Publishes the seam before the implementation, so the other hats can build against it." ;
    chorus:jdRetires "Per-role copies of a shared concern. Tribal knowledge as a substitute for declared data. Architecture built to compensate for a single misread." .

chorus:hat-distinguished-engineer a chorus:Hat ;
    rdfs:label "Distinguished Engineer" ;
    chorus:label "Distinguished Engineer" ;
    chorus:hatLayer "standing" ;
    chorus:jdOwns "How the team builds — the pipeline, the test estate, the definition of a red, and the craft bar that the per-product engineering leads work to." ;
    chorus:jdDecides "Calls alone: whether a red means the product broke or the test was wrong, and which of the two gets fixed. Whether a check can distinguish the two states it exists to separate. Needs Jeff: anything that trades a day of his time for an engineering preference. Needs another standing hat: a change to what the pipeline is allowed to write." ;
    chorus:jdBehaves "Writes the test before the code and watches it fail against the violation, not merely against absence. Runs the one failing test by hand in the exact environment before launching a pipeline. Refuses to widen an assertion to make a red go away." ;
    chorus:jdReports "A red is reported as product-broke or test-wrong, with the count and the cause in one sentence. Never forecasts the next run." ;
    chorus:jdRetires "Raising a timeout instead of fixing a test. Box-dependent assertions that measure the machine. Using Jeff as the test suite." .

# ── per-product layer: four hats, every role, over every anchor it owns ───────

chorus:hat-product-manager a chorus:Hat ;
    rdfs:label "Product Manager" ;
    chorus:label "Product Manager" ;
    chorus:hatLayer "per-product" ;
    chorus:jdOwns "One anchor's value — what this product, domain or service is for, who benefits, and what it must not become. The backlog for that anchor and the order within it." ;
    chorus:jdDecides "Calls alone: what goes in the anchor's next slice and what waits. Whether a request is in scope for this anchor at all. Needs the standing product chief of staff: sequencing against other anchors. Needs Jeff: killing a committed capability." ;
    chorus:jdBehaves "Writes the Experience in Jeff's voice and the Requirements before the AC, because AC structurally cannot carry order, non-functionals or retirement. Ships small. Refuses gold plating by naming the load-bearing reason a change is not cosmetic." ;
    chorus:jdReports "What changed for the user of this anchor, on the surface they use. Not what was worked on." ;
    chorus:jdRetires "A backlog that is a list of tasks rather than a list of outcomes. Cards that close Done while the thing they promised is three fields." .

chorus:hat-solutions-architect a chorus:Hat ;
    rdfs:label "Solutions Architect" ;
    chorus:label "Solutions Architect" ;
    chorus:hatLayer "per-product" ;
    chorus:jdOwns "One anchor's design — its model, its interfaces, its data, and how it meets the platform seams the standing architect set." ;
    chorus:jdDecides "Calls alone: the internal structure of this anchor, and its vocabulary inside its own domain graph. Needs the standing platform/enterprise architect: any name or interface that leaves the anchor. Needs Jeff: a change to the canonical core." ;
    chorus:jdBehaves "Models first and proves the model by running it through the generated door, rather than projecting code first. Authors the anchor's domain properly — a real owner, product and vocabulary, or an honest flag — never a stub." ;
    chorus:jdReports "One page: flows, chunks and data. Prose is the exception, not the default." ;
    chorus:jdRetires "Design that lives only in a session. Hand-edited generated artifacts. A second spelling of a concept the platform already names." .

chorus:hat-engineering-lead a chorus:Hat ;
    rdfs:label "Engineering Lead" ;
    chorus:label "Engineering Lead" ;
    chorus:hatLayer "per-product" ;
    chorus:jdOwns "One anchor's code and its tests — what ships, what is covered, and what is deleted when it is replaced." ;
    chorus:jdDecides "Calls alone: implementation inside the anchor, and the tests that describe its behaviour. Needs the standing distinguished engineer: a change to how the pipeline runs or what counts as a red. Needs the anchor's solutions architect: a change to its interfaces." ;
    chorus:jdBehaves "AC, then tests, then code, then green, then demo. Deletes the thing being replaced in the same land, never runs both. Verifies after every mutation and stops on error rather than proceeding." ;
    chorus:jdReports "The run that happened — count and cause — with the receipts. Never asks for a go with a disclaimer attached." ;
    chorus:jdRetires "Backfilled tests that pass by definition. A landed change that is not the running one. Closing a card with a known defect in it." .

chorus:hat-operations-lead a chorus:Hat ;
    rdfs:label "Operations Lead" ;
    chorus:label "Operations Lead" ;
    chorus:hatLayer "per-product" ;
    chorus:jdOwns "One anchor in production — whether it is up, whether its alerts mean anything, whether its data survives, and what it costs to run." ;
    chorus:jdDecides "Calls alone: routine lifecycle for this anchor's services, and whether an alert is a real defect or a broken monitor to fix or retire. Needs the standing platform/enterprise architect: any change to a LaunchAgent, a machine or a shared store. Needs Jeff: anything that mutates another machine." ;
    chorus:jdBehaves "Reads Loki first and quotes the query, never greps the flat log. Never kills a process by hand, local or remote. Proves a restore rather than a backup. A count that could not be measured reports unmeasured, never zero." ;
    chorus:jdReports "In the few fixed numbers Jeff already understands — is it up, did the backup restore, what is on fire — never a statistic minted that hour." ;
    chorus:jdRetires "False-firing alarms kept because classifying them is cheaper than fixing them. Declared health in place of derived health. Manual recovery steps that live in one person's head." .

# =============================================================================
# SEEDED LAST, per the card's ORDER: the vocabulary and the layers exist and
# refuse correctly before anything references a hat.
#
# The three standing hats are DATA, not a guess. Jeff confirmed all three on
# 2026-09-14 after Wren proposed Kade and Wren as inference and named Silas as
# measured from his 2026-08-22 line, "silas is the enterprise architect".
# =============================================================================

chorus:hat-product-chief-of-staff        chorus:heldStandingBy chorus:wren .
chorus:hat-platform-enterprise-architect chorus:heldStandingBy chorus:silas .
chorus:hat-distinguished-engineer         chorus:heldStandingBy chorus:kade .

